d services, no new network exposure, healthy package management, and only ordinary reboot-related temporary-directory and process-ID changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fcc78802f8a4232","cmd-1ae5fe124fba4303","cmd-dd88a5c4e7814a38","cmd-a996681fccef4cc1","cmd-5829f537eb924512","cmd-a45aa0ea57844760","cmd-365dfbb47cbe4629","cmd-377095b602714c7e","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 has a persistent, root-protected AIDE baseline covering /etc, change reporting was demonstrated and cleaned up, and the original RHEL release and kernel were preserved. The mandatory restart protocol is complete: a healthy pre-restart state and acknowledgement were captured, connection loss bounded the disruption, the boot identity changed, and the database, clean AIDE result, services, protected data, and platform recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The direct change-detection probe demonstrated an added /etc file; modification and deletion cases were not separately exercised, although the active NORMAL rules include content hashes and metadata for those cases.","Lifecycle snapshots provide only bounded inventories and do not independently enumerate the installed AIDE files or database; those final states are instead directly evidenced by executor commands."]}
